\title[Bialgebras of representative functions on free monoids]
{Various Bialgebras of Representative Functions on Free Monoids:\\
Duality, Characters, and the Failure of Rationality for Group-Like Series}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Let $X$ be an alphabet, $X^{*}$ the free monoid it generates, and $K$ a field of
characteristic zero. The space $\KX$ of noncommutative polynomials carries two
mutually dual bialgebra structures: the graded, noncommutative, cocommutative
bialgebra $(\KX,\conc,\Delta_{\shuf})$ built from concatenation and the
unshuffle coproduct, and the graded, commutative, co-noncommutative bialgebra
$(\KX,\shuf,\Delta_{\conc})$ built from the shuffle product and the
deconcatenation coproduct. We develop both from first principles in the
multiset formulation, prove the duality tying them together, prove both
bialgebra axioms, and determine the characters and infinitesimal characters of
each.

Our main results are: (i) \emph{shuffle/unshuffle duality}, the multiplicity of
$w$ in $u \shuf v$ equals the multiplicity of $(u,v)$ in $\Delta_{\shuf}(w)$;
(ii) the \emph{bialgebra axiom for the shuffle structure},
$\Delta_{\conc}(u\shuf v)=\Delta_{\conc}(u)\shuftwo\Delta_{\conc}(v)$, proved by
transporting the computation through duality, where it reduces to a fourfold
transposition of counting sums; (iii) \emph{character theorems}: the characters
of the concatenation bialgebra are exactly the Kleene stars $\ell^{*}$ of planes
$\ell=\sum_x c_x x$, its infinitesimal characters are exactly the planes,
exponentials $\exp(\ell)$ are characters of the shuffle algebra, and every
shuffle character satisfies the divided-power identity $f(a^{n})=f(a)^{n}/n!$;
(iv) the \emph{Kleene--Schützenberger equivalence} between being representative,
having a finite-dimensional space of left translates, and admitting a linear
representation $f(w)=\lambda\mu(w)\gamma$, together with closure of the
representative functions under sums, scalars, Hadamard products and shuffle
products; (v) a \emph{separation theorem}: over $\mathbb{R}$, for a plane $\ell$
with $c_a\neq0$, the series $\exp(\ell)$ is a shuffle character which is
\emph{not} representative, its Hankel matrix along a single letter being
$[1/(m+n)!]$, of infinite rank, whereas $\ell^{*}$ is representative of rank one
but is a shuffle character only if $\ell=0$. The two character groups therefore
meet exactly in the counit.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{shuffle product, unshuffle coproduct, deconcatenation, bialgebra
duality, representative functions, rational noncommutative series,
Kleene--Schützenberger theorem, Hankel rank, group-like series, divided powers}

