\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Two products on one space}
Words over an alphabet $X$ admit two structurally different multiplications.
The first is \emph{concatenation}, $(u,v)\mapsto uv$: associative, unital with
the empty word $1$, and free. The second is the \emph{shuffle},
$(u,v)\mapsto u \shuf v$, the formal sum of all interleavings of $u$ and $v$
preserving the internal order of each factor. The shuffle is associative
\emph{and commutative}, which is remarkable on objects whose defining feature is
that order matters.

Both live on the same vector space $\KX=\bigoplus_{w\in X^{*}}K\,w$, and each
pairs with a coproduct to form a bialgebra. Concatenation pairs with the
\emph{unshuffle} coproduct $\Delta_{\shuf}$, giving a graded noncommutative
cocommutative bialgebra; the shuffle pairs with the \emph{deconcatenation}
coproduct $\Delta_{\conc}$, giving a graded commutative co-noncommutative
bialgebra. The two are graded duals of each other: in the inner product on
$\KX$ for which distinct words are orthonormal, the shuffle is the transpose of
the unshuffle and concatenation is the transpose of deconcatenation.

\subsection{Representative functions and rationality}
Independently, there is a computational notion. A function $f:X^{*}\to K$ is
\emph{representative} when $(u,v)\mapsto f(uv)$ splits as a finite sum
$\sum_{i<n}g_i(u)h_i(v)$. This is precisely the condition that $f$ can be
evaluated by a finite weighted automaton and, by the Kleene--Schützenberger
theorem, precisely the condition that the \emph{graph} $\sum_w f(w)w$ of $f$ is
a \emph{rational} noncommutative series, i.e.\ admits a linear representation
$f(w)=\lambda\mu(w)\gamma$ for a monoid morphism $\mu:X^{*}\to M_n(K)$.

The theme of this paper is the interaction of these two circles of ideas.
Rational series are stable under the algebraic operations coming from
\emph{both} bialgebra structures; but the \emph{characters} of the two
structures --- their group-like elements --- behave in radically different ways.

Throughout, $K$ is a field, of characteristic zero wherever factorials are
inverted; $X$ is an arbitrary alphabet; $\abs{w}$ is the length of $w$; and $1$
is the empty word.

\section{The shuffle product of words}

\begin{definition}[Shuffle]\label{def:shuffle}
The shuffle $u \shuf v$ of two words is the multiset of words defined by
\[
1 \shuf v=\{v\},\qquad u \shuf 1=\{u\},\qquad
(au)\shuf(bv)=a\cdot\bigl(u \shuf (bv)\bigr)+b\cdot\bigl((au)\shuf v\bigr),
\]
where $a\cdot S$ prefixes $a$ to every element of the multiset $S$ and $+$ is
multiset sum. The recursion terminates since $\abs{u}+\abs{v}$ strictly
decreases.
\end{definition}

Equivalently, $u \shuf v$ enumerates the pairs $(S,S^{c})$ of complementary
subsets of the positions $\{1,\dots,\abs{u}+\abs{v}\}$ with $\abs{S}=\abs{u}$,
the resulting word spelling $u$ on $S$ and $v$ on $S^{c}$. Distinct position
sets contribute distinct terms even when the words coincide: $a \shuf a=\{aa,aa\}$.

\begin{theorem}[Commutativity]\label{thm:comm}
$u \shuf v=v \shuf u$ for all $u,v\in X^{*}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Double induction on $\abs{u},\abs{v}$. The base cases are the unit rules; in the
inductive step both sides expand to the same two-term sum after commuting the
summands.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Grading]\label{thm:grading}
If $z\in u \shuf v$ then $\abs{z}=\abs{u}+\abs{v}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on $\abs{u}+\abs{v}$: each branch prefixes one letter and
reduces the total length by one.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cardinality]\label{thm:card}
$\abs{u \shuf v}=\binom{\abs{u}+\abs{v}}{\abs{u}}$, counted with multiplicity.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction with Definition~\ref{def:shuffle}: the two branches contribute
$\binom{m-1+n}{m-1}$ and $\binom{m+n-1}{m}$ with $m=\abs{u}$, $n=\abs{v}$, and
Pascal's rule assembles $\binom{m+n}{m}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
For a multiset $S$ of words and a word $w$, put
$S \shuf w:=\sum_{z\in S}(z \shuf w)$, the multiset sum over the elements of $S$
with multiplicity.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Associativity]\label{thm:assoc}
$(u \shuf v)\shuf w=u \shuf (v \shuf w)$ for all $u,v,w$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on $\abs{u}+\abs{v}+\abs{w}$. If any argument is empty both
sides collapse by the unit rules and Theorem~\ref{thm:comm}. For
$u=au'$, $v=bv'$, $w=cw'$, expand both sides; each becomes a sum of three terms
indexed by which of $a,b,c$ is emitted first. Matching them uses the induction
hypothesis at three smaller triples together with
\[
\bigl(a\cdot T\bigr)\shuf(cw')=a\cdot\bigl(T \shuf (cw')\bigr)+c\cdot\bigl((a\cdot T)\shuf w'\bigr),
\]
and a rearrangement of the six resulting summands.
\end{proof}

Thus $(\KX,\shuf,1)$ is a commutative, associative, unital, graded $K$-algebra.

\section{The unshuffle coproduct and duality}

\begin{definition}[Unshuffle coproduct]\label{def:unsh}
$\Delta_{\shuf}$ is defined on words by
\[
\Delta_{\shuf}(1)=\{(1,1)\},\qquad
\Delta_{\shuf}(aw)=a\cdot_{L}\Delta_{\shuf}(w)+a\cdot_{R}\Delta_{\shuf}(w),
\]
where $a\cdot_{L}$ prefixes $a$ to the left component of each pair and
$a\cdot_{R}$ to the right. In linear notation,
$\Delta_{\shuf}(aw)=(a\otimes1+1\otimes a)\Delta_{\shuf}(w)$: the unique
concatenation-algebra morphism making every letter primitive.
\end{definition}

Combinatorially, $\Delta_{\shuf}(w)$ enumerates the $2^{\abs{w}}$ two-colourings
of the positions of $w$, recording the subword of each colour. Hence
$\abs{\Delta_{\shuf}(w)}=2^{\abs{w}}$, and every $(u,v)\in\Delta_{\shuf}(w)$
satisfies $\abs{u}+\abs{v}=\abs{w}$.

\begin{theorem}[Shuffle/unshuffle duality]\label{thm:duality}
For all $u,v,w\in X^{*}$,
\[
\mult\bigl(w,\ u \shuf v\bigr)=\mult\bigl((u,v),\ \Delta_{\shuf}(w)\bigr).
\]
Equivalently, in the inner product on $\KX$ making distinct words orthonormal,
$\langle u \shuf v,\,w\rangle=\langle u\otimes v,\,\Delta_{\shuf}(w)\rangle$:
the shuffle product and the unshuffle coproduct are transposes.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $w$ with $u,v$ universally quantified. For $w=1$ both sides are
$1$ if $u=v=1$ and $0$ otherwise. For $w=cw'$ there are four cases according to
whether $u$ and $v$ are empty; in the generic case $u=au'$, $v=bv'$,
\begin{align*}
\mult(cw',\,au'\shuf bv')&=[a=c]\,\mult(w',\,u'\shuf bv')+[b=c]\,\mult(w',\,au'\shuf v'),\\
\mult\bigl((au',bv'),\Delta_{\shuf}(cw')\bigr)&=[a=c]\,\mult\bigl((u',bv'),\Delta_{\shuf}(w')\bigr)
+[b=c]\,\mult\bigl((au',v'),\Delta_{\shuf}(w')\bigr),
\end{align*}
and the right-hand sides agree by the induction hypothesis. Conceptually, both
quantities count the colourings of the positions of $w$ in which colour one
spells $u$ and colour two spells $v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cocommutativity]\label{thm:cocomm}
Swapping the components of every pair leaves $\Delta_{\shuf}(w)$ unchanged.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The recursion is symmetric in $\cdot_{L}$ and $\cdot_{R}$; combinatorially,
complementation $S\mapsto S^{c}$ is an involution on colourings.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Coassociativity]\label{thm:coassoc}
$(\Delta_{\shuf}\otimes\mathrm{id})\circ\Delta_{\shuf}
=(\mathrm{id}\otimes\Delta_{\shuf})\circ\Delta_{\shuf}$
as multisets of triples of words.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both iterates satisfy
$\Delta^{(2)}(aw)=(a\otimes1\otimes1+1\otimes a\otimes1+1\otimes1\otimes a)\Delta^{(2)}(w)$;
a one-step induction concludes. Combinatorially, both count three-colourings of
the positions of $w$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bialgebra axiom for concatenation]\label{thm:bialg1}
For all $u,v$,
\[
\Delta_{\shuf}(uv)=\Delta_{\shuf}(u)\cdot\Delta_{\shuf}(v),
\]
the product being componentwise concatenation of tensors,
$(p_1\otimes p_2)(q_1\otimes q_2)=p_1q_1\otimes p_2q_2$, extended bilinearly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $u$. For $u=1$ both sides equal $\Delta_{\shuf}(v)$. For $u=au'$
apply Definition~\ref{def:unsh}, the induction hypothesis and distributivity of
the componentwise product over multiset sums; the branches match term for term.
Combinatorially: a two-colouring of the positions of $uv$ is a two-colouring of
those of $u$ together with one of those of $v$.
\end{proof}

With the counit $\varepsilon(w)=[\,w=1\,]$ we obtain:

\begin{corollary}
$(\KX,\conc,\Delta_{\shuf},\varepsilon)$ is a graded, noncommutative,
cocommutative bialgebra, connected in degree $0$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The deconcatenation coproduct and the dual bialgebra}

\begin{definition}[Deconcatenation]
$\Delta_{\conc}(w)=\sum_{w=z_1z_2}z_1\otimes z_2$, i.e.\ the multiset
$\{(w_{<k},w_{\ge k}):0\le k\le\abs{w}\}$. Recursively,
$\Delta_{\conc}(1)=\{(1,1)\}$ and
$\Delta_{\conc}(aw)=(1\otimes aw)+a\cdot_{L}\Delta_{\conc}(w)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Coefficients]\label{lem:deconc}
$\abs{\Delta_{\conc}(w)}=\abs{w}+1$, and the multiplicity of $(z_1,z_2)$ in
$\Delta_{\conc}(z)$ is $1$ if $z_1z_2=z$ and $0$ otherwise.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $z$, splitting on whether $z_1$ is empty; cancellation in the free
monoid gives uniqueness of the cut point.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Shuffle on the tensor square]
$(p_1\otimes p_2)\shuftwo(q_1\otimes q_2):=(p_1\shuf q_1)\otimes(p_2\shuf q_2)$,
extended bilinearly.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Bialgebra axiom for the shuffle structure]\label{thm:bialg2}
For all words $u,v$,
\[
\Delta_{\conc}\bigl(u \shuf v\bigr)=\Delta_{\conc}(u)\shuftwo\Delta_{\conc}(v),
\]
the left side meaning: apply $\Delta_{\conc}$ to each element of the multiset
$u \shuf v$ and take the multiset sum.
\end{theorem}

The direct combinatorial proof is unpleasant, since the two sides are sums over
index sets of different shapes. We prove it by duality, which converts the
statement into a transposition of summation order, using the following identity
of independent interest.

\begin{lemma}[Fourfold transposition]\label{lem:quad}
Let $P$ be a multiset of pairs in $A\times B$, $Q$ in $C\times D$, $R$ in
$A\times C$, $S$ in $B\times D$. Then
\[
\sum_{p\in P}\sum_{q\in Q}\mult\bigl((p_1,q_1),R\bigr)\,\mult\bigl((p_2,q_2),S\bigr)
=\sum_{r\in R}\sum_{s\in S}\mult\bigl((r_1,s_1),P\bigr)\,\mult\bigl((r_2,s_2),Q\bigr).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Replace each multiplicity by a sum of indicators, $\mult(x,R)=\sum_{r\in R}[x=r]$.
Both sides become the same quadruple sum
\[
\sum_{p\in P}\sum_{q\in Q}\sum_{r\in R}\sum_{s\in S}
[p_1=r_1][q_1=r_2][p_2=s_1][q_2=s_2],
\]
after noting that $[(p_1,q_1)=r]\,[(p_2,q_2)=s]=[(r_1,s_1)=p]\,[(r_2,s_2)=q]$:
both equal $1$ exactly when the four coordinate equalities hold. Interchanging
the four finite summations completes the proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch of Theorem~\ref{thm:bialg2}]
Fix $z_1\otimes z_2$ and compute its multiplicity on both sides.

\emph{Left side.} By Lemma~\ref{lem:deconc},
\[
\mult\bigl((z_1,z_2),\Delta_{\conc}(u \shuf v)\bigr)
=\sum_{z\in u \shuf v}[\,z_1z_2=z\,]=\mult\bigl(z_1z_2,\ u \shuf v\bigr),
\]
which by Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} equals
$\mult\bigl((u,v),\Delta_{\shuf}(z_1z_2)\bigr)$ and by Theorem~\ref{thm:bialg1}
equals $\mult\bigl((u,v),\Delta_{\shuf}(z_1)\Delta_{\shuf}(z_2)\bigr)$; expanding,
\[
=\sum_{\alpha\in\Delta_{\shuf}(z_1)}\ \sum_{\beta\in\Delta_{\shuf}(z_2)}
[\,\alpha_1\beta_1=u\,]\,[\,\alpha_2\beta_2=v\,].
\]

\emph{Right side.} By definition and Theorem~\ref{thm:duality},
\[
\sum_{p\in\Delta_{\conc}(u)}\sum_{q\in\Delta_{\conc}(v)}
\mult\bigl((p_1,q_1),\Delta_{\shuf}(z_1)\bigr)\,
\mult\bigl((p_2,q_2),\Delta_{\shuf}(z_2)\bigr),
\]
and Lemma~\ref{lem:quad} with $P=\Delta_{\conc}(u)$, $Q=\Delta_{\conc}(v)$,
$R=\Delta_{\shuf}(z_1)$, $S=\Delta_{\shuf}(z_2)$ rewrites this as
\[
\sum_{\alpha\in\Delta_{\shuf}(z_1)}\sum_{\beta\in\Delta_{\shuf}(z_2)}
\mult\bigl((\alpha_1,\beta_1),\Delta_{\conc}(u)\bigr)\,
\mult\bigl((\alpha_2,\beta_2),\Delta_{\conc}(v)\bigr),
\]
which by Lemma~\ref{lem:deconc} is the left-side expression.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$(\KX,\shuf,\Delta_{\conc},\varepsilon)$ is a graded, commutative,
co-noncommutative bialgebra, the graded dual of $(\KX,\conc,\Delta_{\shuf})$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Characters and infinitesimal characters}

Throughout, $\varepsilon(w)=[\,w=1\,]$ and a linear form on $\KX$ is identified
with a function $f:X^{*}\to K$.

\subsection{The concatenation side}

\begin{definition}
$f:X^{*}\to K$ is a \emph{character of the concatenation bialgebra} if $f(1)=1$
and $f(uv)=f(u)f(v)$ for all $u,v$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Planes and Kleene stars]
A \emph{plane} is a linear form supported in degree one, i.e.\ a family
$(c_x)_{x\in X}$, written $\ell=\sum_x c_x x$. Its \emph{Kleene star} is
$\ell^{*}=\sum_{n\ge0}\ell^{n}$, with coefficient function
$\ell^{*}(x_1\cdots x_n)=c_{x_1}\cdots c_{x_n}$ and $\ell^{*}(1)=1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Characters are Kleene stars of planes]\label{thm:char-conc}
$f$ is a character of the concatenation bialgebra if and only if $f=\ell^{*}$
for a unique plane $\ell$, namely $c_x=f(x)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) The product of letter-values telescopes over a concatenation.
($\Rightarrow$) Induction: $f(1)=1$, and $f(aw)=f(a)f(w)=c_a\ell^{*}(w)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
$g:X^{*}\to K$ is an \emph{infinitesimal character} of the concatenation
bialgebra if $g(uv)=g(u)\varepsilon(v)+\varepsilon(u)g(v)$ for all $u,v$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Infinitesimal characters are exactly the planes]\label{thm:infchar}
$g$ is an infinitesimal character if and only if $g(w)=0$ for every word $w$
with $\abs{w}\neq1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) Taking $u=v=1$ gives $g(1)=2g(1)$, so $g(1)=0$; if
$\abs{w}\ge2$, write $w=uv$ with $u,v$ nonempty, so
$\varepsilon(u)=\varepsilon(v)=0$ and $g(w)=0$. ($\Leftarrow$) Check by cases:
if $u=1$ or $v=1$ both sides agree using $g(1)=0$; if both are nonempty then
$\abs{uv}\ge2$ and all three terms vanish.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:char-conc} and~\ref{thm:infchar} are the statement that, for
concatenation, only the Kleene stars of planes are characters, or equivalently
only the planes are infinitesimal characters --- a Ree-type pair.

\subsection{The shuffle side}

\begin{definition}
$f$ is a \emph{character of the shuffle algebra} if $f(1)=1$ and
$f(u)f(v)=\sum_{z\in u \shuf v}f(z)$ (sum with multiplicity). By
Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} this says exactly that $\sum_w f(w)w$ is
\emph{group-like} for $\Delta_{\shuf}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Shuffles preserve letter weights]\label{lem:weights}
For any family $(c_x)$ and any $z\in u \shuf v$, the product of the $c$-values
along $z$ equals the product along $u$ times the product along $v$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on the shuffle recursion: each step prefixes one letter drawn from one
factor, multiplying the running product by its $c$-value.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exponentials of planes are shuffle characters]\label{thm:exp}
Let $\operatorname{char}K=0$ and $\ell=\sum_x c_x x$ a plane. Then
\[
\exp(\ell)(x_1\cdots x_n)=\frac{c_{x_1}\cdots c_{x_n}}{n!}
\]
is a character of the shuffle algebra.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $\abs{u}=m$, $\abs{v}=n$ and $C_u$ the product of $c$-values along $u$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:grading} every $z\in u \shuf v$ has length $m+n$, and by
Lemma~\ref{lem:weights} every such $z$ has weight $C_uC_v$. Hence, using
Theorem~\ref{thm:card} and $\binom{m+n}{m}m!\,n!=(m+n)!$,
\[
\sum_{z\in u \shuf v}\exp(\ell)(z)=\binom{m+n}{m}\frac{C_uC_v}{(m+n)!}
=\frac{C_u}{m!}\cdot\frac{C_v}{n!}. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:divpow-shuffle}
$a^{n}\shuf a=(n+1)\cdot a^{n+1}$ as multisets.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction: $a^{n+1}\shuf a=a\cdot(a^{n}\shuf a)+a\cdot(a^{n+1}\shuf1)$, giving
$(n+1)+1$ copies of $a^{n+2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Divided powers]\label{thm:divpow}
Let $\operatorname{char}K=0$ and let $f$ be any character of the shuffle
algebra. Then $f(a^{n})=f(a)^{n}/n!$ for every letter $a$ and every $n\ge0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$; the case $n=0$ is $f(1)=1$. For the step, apply the character
identity to $u=a^{n}$, $v=a$ and use Lemma~\ref{lem:divpow-shuffle}:
$f(a^{n})f(a)=(n+1)f(a^{n+1})$, whence $f(a^{n+1})=f(a)^{n+1}/(n+1)!$.
\end{proof}

Thus a shuffle character is rigid along each single letter: it must agree there
with the exponential of the plane determined by its values on letters. This is
the germ of the separation theorem.

\section{Representative functions and the Kleene--Schützenberger theorem}

Let $K$ be a field.

\begin{definition}[Translates]
For $w\in X^{*}$ the \emph{left translate} of $f$ is $w^{-1}f:u\mapsto f(wu)$;
the \emph{translate space} $T(f)\subseteq K^{X^{*}}$ is the span of
$\{w^{-1}f:w\in X^{*}\}$. Its dimension is the \emph{Hankel rank} of $f$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Representative]
$f$ is \emph{representative} if there are $n\in\mathbb{N}$ and functions
$g_1,\dots,g_n,h_1,\dots,h_n:X^{*}\to K$ with
$f(uv)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}g_i(u)h_i(v)$ for all $u,v$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Linear representation]
$f$ \emph{admits a linear representation} of dimension $n$ if there are
$\lambda\in K^{1\times n}$, $\gamma\in K^{n\times1}$ and $\mu:X\to M_n(K)$,
extended by $\mu(1)=I$ and $\mu(aw)=\mu(a)\mu(w)$, with
$f(w)=\lambda\mu(w)\gamma$ for all $w$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Kleene--Schützenberger for representative functions]\label{thm:KS}
For $f:X^{*}\to K$ the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f$ is representative;
\item $T(f)$ is finite dimensional;
\item $f$ admits a linear representation.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1)$\Rightarrow$(2): from $f(uv)=\sum_i g_i(u)h_i(v)$ we get
$w^{-1}f=\sum_i g_i(w)h_i$, so $T(f)$ lies in the span of $h_1,\dots,h_n$.

(2)$\Rightarrow$(3): the Myhill--Nerode construction. Let $V=T(f)$ have
dimension $n$ and basis $(e_1,\dots,e_n)$. Then $f=1^{-1}f\in V$, and $V$ is
stable under each shift $\sigma_a:g\mapsto a^{-1}g$, since the shift of a
generator is a generator, $\sigma_a(w^{-1}f)=(wa)^{-1}f$, and shifts are linear.
Let $\mu(a)$ be the matrix of $\sigma_a$ in the basis; then $\mu$ extends to a
monoid morphism, the coordinate vector of $w^{-1}f$ is $\lambda\mu(w)$ with
$\lambda$ the coordinate vector of $f$, and with $\gamma_i=e_i(1)$ evaluation at
the empty word gives $\lambda\mu(w)\gamma=(w^{-1}f)(1)=f(w)$.

(3)$\Rightarrow$(1): $f(uv)=\lambda\mu(u)\mu(v)\gamma=\sum_i(\lambda\mu(u))_i(\mu(v)\gamma)_i$.
\end{proof}

Identifying $f$ with its graph $\sum_w f(w)w$, condition (3) is rationality in
the sense of Kleene--Schützenberger; representative functions are exactly the
rational series.

\begin{proposition}[Closure properties]\label{prop:closure}
The representative functions form a unital subalgebra of $K^{X^{*}}$ for the
pointwise operations: they are closed under sums (size $n+m$), scalar multiples,
and Hadamard products (size $nm$); constants are representative of size $1$; and
every monoid morphism $f:X^{*}\to(K,\cdot)$ --- in particular every Kleene star
of a plane --- is representative of size $1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Sums: concatenate the two families of factors. Scalars: scale one family.
Hadamard: tensor the two factorizations, which on representations is the
Kronecker product $\mu\otimes\nu$.
\end{proof}

\section{The shuffle product of series and stability of rationality}

\begin{definition}\label{def:shufseries}
For $f,g:X^{*}\to K$ set
$(f \shuf g)(w)=\sum_{(u,v)\in\Delta_{\shuf}(w)}f(u)g(v)$, the sum over the
multiset of $2^{\abs{w}}$ pairs.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Consistency with the shuffle of words]
Let $\delta_u$ be the indicator of $u$. Then
$(\delta_u \shuf \delta_v)(w)=\mult(w,\ u \shuf v)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Only the term $(u,v)$ contributes, with its multiplicity in $\Delta_{\shuf}(w)$,
which by Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} is $\mult(w,u \shuf v)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
$(K^{X^{*}},\shuf,\varepsilon)$ is a commutative, associative, unital
$K$-algebra: commutativity follows from cocommutativity
(Theorem~\ref{thm:cocomm}), unitality because the only pair of
$\Delta_{\shuf}(w)$ with empty first component is $(1,w)$, occurring once, and
associativity from coassociativity (Theorem~\ref{thm:coassoc}), both sides being
$\sum_{(p,q,r)}f(p)g(q)h(r)$ over the iterated coproduct.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Rationality is preserved by the shuffle]\label{thm:shufrational}
If $f,g$ are representative, so is $f \shuf g$; if $f$ has a factorization of
size $n$ and $g$ one of size $m$, then $f \shuf g$ has one of size $nm$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $f(uv)=\sum_{i<n}a_i(u)b_i(v)$ and $g(uv)=\sum_{j<m}c_j(u)d_j(v)$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:bialg1} every pair splitting $uv$ is uniquely
$(\alpha_1\beta_1,\alpha_2\beta_2)$ with $\alpha\in\Delta_{\shuf}(u)$,
$\beta\in\Delta_{\shuf}(v)$, multiplicities multiplying. Substituting and
exchanging the finite sum over $(i,j)$ with the two multiset sums gives
\[
(f \shuf g)(uv)=\sum_{i<n}\sum_{j<m}\bigl(a_i \shuf c_j\bigr)(u)\,
\bigl(b_i \shuf d_j\bigr)(v),
\]
a factorization of size $nm$.
\end{proof}

Together with Proposition~\ref{prop:closure}, the representative functions form
a subalgebra of $K^{X^{*}}$ for the Hadamard product \emph{and} for the shuffle
product.

\section{The separation theorem}

We work over $\mathbb{R}$; the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:hankel} uses the
Archimedean property.

\begin{lemma}[Factorial ratio bound]\label{lem:ratio}
For $i\ge k+1$ and all $n$, $\dfrac{(n+k)!}{(n+i)!}\le\dfrac{1}{n+k+1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(n+i)!\ge(n+k+1)!=(n+k+1)(n+k)!$ by monotonicity of factorials.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Independence of the factorial Hankel rows]\label{thm:hankel}
Let $N\in\mathbb{N}$ and $g_0,\dots,g_N\in\mathbb{R}$ satisfy
$\sum_{i=0}^{N}g_i\,\frac{n!}{(n+i)!}=0$ for every $n\ge0$. Then $g_i=0$ for
all $i$. Equivalently, the infinite Hankel matrix $[\,1/(m+n)!\,]_{m,n\ge0}$ has
infinite rank.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on $k$: assume $g_i=0$ for $i<k$. Rescaling the hypothesis by
$(n+k)!/n!$ gives $\sum_{i}g_i\frac{(n+k)!}{(n+i)!}=0$ for every $n$. Terms with
$i<k$ vanish, the term $i=k$ equals $g_k$, and each term with $i>k$ is bounded
by $\abs{g_i}/(n+k+1)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:ratio}. With $M=\sum_i\abs{g_i}$ we get
$\abs{g_k}\le M/(n+k+1)$ for every $n$, so $g_k=0$ by the Archimedean property.
\end{proof}

Concretely, the determinants of the leading $n\times n$ blocks of
$[1/(i+j)!]$ for $n=1,\dots,7$ are
\[
1,\ -\tfrac12,\ -\tfrac1{144},\ \tfrac1{1036800},\ \tfrac1{1463132160000},\
-\tfrac1{668986161758208000000},\ -\tfrac1{148045794139338685651353600000000},
\]
all nonzero.

\begin{theorem}[Separation]\label{thm:sep}
Let $a\in X$ and let $\ell=\sum_x c_x x$ be a plane over $\mathbb{R}$ with
$c_a\neq0$. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\exp(\ell)$ is a character of the shuffle algebra;
\item $\exp(\ell)$ is \emph{not} representative: its graph is not rational;
\item $\ell^{*}$ is representative, of rank one.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) is Theorem~\ref{thm:exp}, (3) is Proposition~\ref{prop:closure}. For (2),
suppose $f=\exp(\ell)$ were representative; by Theorem~\ref{thm:KS} the space
$T(f)$ has some finite dimension $N$, so the $N+1$ translates
$(a^{i})^{-1}f$, $i\le N$, are linearly dependent: $\sum_i g_i (a^{i})^{-1}f=0$
with not all $g_i$ zero. Evaluating at $a^{n}$ and using
$f(a^{k})=c_a^{k}/k!$ gives $\sum_{i}g_i c_a^{i+n}/(i+n)!=0$ for every $n$.
Multiplying by $n!/c_a^{n}$ and setting $G_i=g_ic_a^{i}$ yields exactly the
hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:hankel}, so all $G_i=0$, hence all $g_i=0$: a
contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Kleene stars are almost never shuffle characters]\label{thm:sep2}
Let $K$ be a field and $\ell=\sum_x c_x x$ a plane. If $\ell^{*}$ is a character
of the shuffle algebra then $c_x=0$ for every $x$, i.e.\ $\ell=0$ and
$\ell^{*}=\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the character identity to $u=v=x$: since $x \shuf x=2\cdot xx$ and
$\ell^{*}(xx)=c_x^{2}$, we get $c_x^{2}=2c_x^{2}$, so $c_x=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The character group of the concatenation bialgebra and the character group of
the shuffle algebra, both inside $K^{X^{*}}$, intersect exactly in the counit
$\varepsilon$. Over $\mathbb{R}$, no nontrivial shuffle character is rational,
while every concatenation character is rational of rank one.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms and computational illustration}

\subsection{Shuffle enumeration}
Definition~\ref{def:shuffle} computes $u \shuf v$ directly; the naive cost is
output-dominated, $\Theta\bigl(\binom{m+n}{m}(m+n)\bigr)$, but memoizing on
suffix pairs reduces the number of distinct recursive calls to $(m+1)(n+1)$.

\subsection{Coproducts}
$\Delta_{\shuf}(w)$ is computed by the recursion of Definition~\ref{def:unsh},
with $2^{\abs{w}}$ output terms; $\Delta_{\conc}(w)$ costs $O(\abs{w}^{2})$
naively and $O(\abs{w})$ with shared suffixes.

\subsection{Hankel rank}
Given an oracle for $f$, form $H_d=[f(uv)]$ indexed by all words of length
$\le d$ and compute its rank by exact Gaussian elimination over $\mathbb{Q}$.
By Theorem~\ref{thm:KS}, $f$ is representative iff $\operatorname{rank}H_d$ is
bounded as $d\to\infty$, the bound being the minimal dimension of a linear
representation. For $\abs{X}=q$ the matrix has $(q^{d+1}-1)/(q-1)$ rows, and
elimination costs $O(\text{size}^{3})$ field operations. This is the
computational face of Theorem~\ref{thm:sep}: for $f=\exp(\ell)$ on one letter
the ranks are $2,3,4,5,6,\dots$, while for $f=\ell^{*}$ they are constantly $1$.

\subsection{Verified numerical checks}
Exact rational computation confirms, on all words up to the stated lengths:
the cardinality formula, commutativity and associativity of the shuffle for
$\abs{u},\abs{v},\abs{w}\le3$ on two letters; duality, cocommutativity,
coassociativity and $\Delta_{\shuf}(uv)=\Delta_{\shuf}(u)\Delta_{\shuf}(v)$;
the dual axiom $\Delta_{\conc}(u \shuf v)=\Delta_{\conc}(u)\shuftwo\Delta_{\conc}(v)$;
that $\ell^{*}$ is a concatenation but not a shuffle character while
$\exp(\ell)$ is a shuffle character, with divided powers up to $n=5$; that
$f(w)=\abs{w}_a$ has the representation $\lambda=(1,0)$,
$\mu(a)=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$,
$\mu(b)=I$, $\gamma=(0,1)^{\top}$ with Hankel ranks $2,2,2$, and that its
shuffle with $g(w)=2^{\abs{w}}$ again has ranks $2,2,2$; and that $\exp(a)$ has
ranks $2,3,4,5,6$ at depths $1,\dots,5$.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Why duality is the efficient route}
The two bialgebra axioms have very different direct proofs. On the concatenation
side both sides of Theorem~\ref{thm:bialg1} are recursively generated in the same
variable, and a one-line induction suffices. On the shuffle side the two sides of
Theorem~\ref{thm:bialg2} recurse on incompatible data --- the shuffle on one side,
the cut points on the other. Duality repairs this: reading each side
coefficientwise and applying Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} turns both into quadruple
sums of indicator products over the same four index multisets, differing only in
summation order. This explains \emph{why} the axiom holds: it is the transpose of
an axiom that is nearly definitional.

\subsection{The shape of the dichotomy}
The rational functions are stable under sums, scalars, Hadamard products and
shuffle products. The two character families are each rigidly determined by
their degree-one data. Yet they are transverse: the Kleene stars are rational of
rank one, the exponentials are irrational of infinite rank, and they overlap only
at $\varepsilon$. The mechanism is a single arithmetic fact --- shuffle
characters are forced to have divided powers, and $[1/(m+n)!]$ has infinite
Hankel rank while $[c^{m+n}]=(c^{m})(c^{n})$ has rank one.

\subsection{Iterated integrals and signatures}
The shuffle algebra is the algebra of iterated integrals: the coefficients of a
path signature multiply by the shuffle rule and the signature is group-like for
$\Delta_{\shuf}$. Theorem~\ref{thm:sep} then says that a nondegenerate signature
is never a rational series: its coefficient function cannot be produced by a
finite weighted automaton. The factorial decay of signature coefficients --- the
property that makes truncated signatures effective features in data analysis ---
is precisely the obstruction to finite-state computability.

\subsection{Scope and limitations}
First, our shuffle-side character theorem is complete only in the one-letter
direction: Theorem~\ref{thm:divpow} pins down every shuffle character along the
powers of a single letter but does not classify all shuffle characters over a
multi-letter alphabet, which requires the Lie-element machinery. Second, the
separation argument is Archimedean and stated over $\mathbb{R}$; extending it to
an arbitrary field of characteristic zero requires replacing
Theorem~\ref{thm:hankel} by an algebraic nonvanishing argument for the minors of
$[1/(m+n)!]$.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Antipode of the shuffle Hopf algebra]
For every nonempty word $w$ and every field $K\supseteq\mathbb{Q}$,
\[
\sum_{(u,v)\in\Delta_{\conc}(w)}(-1)^{\abs{u}}\ \mult\bigl(z,\ \widetilde{u}\shuf v\bigr)=0
\qquad\text{for every }z,
\]
where $\widetilde{u}$ is the reversal of $u$; that is,
$S(w)=(-1)^{\abs{w}}\widetilde{w}$ is the antipode of the graded Hopf algebra
$(\KX,\shuf,\Delta_{\conc})$.
\end{conjecture}

The bialgebra axiom of Theorem~\ref{thm:bialg2} upgrades to a Hopf structure
precisely because $\Delta_{\conc}$ is connected and graded, so the antipode is
forced by Takeuchi's recursion; the closed form must then be a signed multiset
identity between shuffles of reversals, a purely combinatorial claim accessible
to the counting machinery developed here. The antipode is the last missing
structure map and is exactly the input needed to state Sweedler duality
intrinsically.

\begin{conjecture}[Shuffle inversion leaves the rational locus]
Theorem~\ref{thm:shufrational} shows rationality is preserved by $\shuf$. The
shuffle-invertible rational series (those with $f(1)\neq0$) do \emph{not} form a
group inside the rational series: there is a rational $f$ with $f(1)=1$ whose
shuffle inverse $g$ --- unique as a series --- has infinite Hankel rank.
\end{conjecture}

The separation of Theorem~\ref{thm:sep} should be reachable from a rational input
by shuffle inversion, since the shuffle inverse of a series of the form $1-x$ is
exponential-like with factorial denominators; Theorem~\ref{thm:hankel} supplies a
working method for proving infinite Hankel rank.

\begin{conjecture}[$q$-deformations preserve the rational locus]
For $q\in K$ define the $q$-shuffle by
\[
au \shuf_q bv = a\,(u \shuf_q bv)+b\,(au \shuf_q v)+q\,[\,a=b\,]\ ab\,(u \shuf_q v).
\]
For every $q$ the rational series form a subalgebra for $\shuf_q$, and the
associated character group varies with $q$ while remaining disjoint from the
rational locus except at the counit.
\end{conjecture}

Three further directions suggest themselves: (a) identifying the commutative
co-noncommutative bialgebra of series intrinsically as the Sweedler dual of the
graded concatenation bialgebra, reading Theorem~\ref{thm:char-conc} as the
statement that only Kleene stars of planes are characters; (b) sharpening the
bound $nm$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:shufrational} on the Hankel rank of $f \shuf g$;
(c) positive characteristic, where divided-power algebras replace exponentials
and the separation theorem should have an analogue with a different arithmetic
mechanism.

\section{Conclusion}

We have developed the two mutually dual bialgebra structures carried by
noncommutative polynomials over a free monoid, established the duality between
the shuffle product and the unshuffle coproduct, and used that duality to give
an economical proof of the bialgebra axiom for the shuffle/deconcatenation
structure. We determined the characters and infinitesimal characters of the
concatenation structure --- Kleene stars of planes and planes respectively ---
and showed that shuffle characters obey forced divided powers. Placing these
families against the Kleene--Schützenberger criterion, we proved that although
rational series are stable under both the Hadamard and the shuffle products, the
two character families are transverse: over $\mathbb{R}$ the exponential of a
nonzero plane is a shuffle character of infinite Hankel rank, hence not
rational, and a Kleene star is a shuffle character only when it is the counit.
The single arithmetic fact underlying the dichotomy is the infinite rank of the
factorial Hankel matrix $[1/(m+n)!]$, itself a consequence of nothing more than
the Archimedean growth of factorials.

